<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_conductors-current" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  	<title>Conductors and Current</title>
<assemblage component="implementation">
  <title>Implementation Notes</title>
  <p>
	The activities in this section were designed to be implemented in a 50-minute interactive classroom, after students have completed the rest of the chapter, especially:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<p>
				<xref ref="sec_potential-app"/>
			</p>
		</li>
		<li>
			<p>
				<xref ref="sec_conductors"/>
			</p>
		</li>
		<li>
			<p>
				<xref ref="sec_electric-current"/>
			</p>
		</li>
	</ul>
  </assemblage>

	<activity>
		<title>Warm-Up</title>
		<statement>
		<p>
			
		</p>
		<p>Suppose you place a positive charge in a cavity inside the conductor below.  Sketch a charge diagram for the conductor.</p>

		<figure xml:id="conductor2">
			  <title>Conductor Figure</title>
			<caption>A positive charge within a cavity in a conductor.</caption>
			<image source="Conductor2.png" width = "50%" ><description>A square conductor with a small square cavity in the bottom right.  A positive point charge lies within the cavity.</description></image>
		</figure>
	</statement>
		
	</activity>
		
   <activity>
	<title>Inside a Capacitor I</title>
	<introduction>
		<p>
			
		</p>
		<p>
			The capacitor shown below is made out of two parallel conducting plates.  Initially, <m>V = 0</m> at point A and <m>V = 9 \mathrm{~V}</m> at point B.
		</p>
		<figure xml:id="Capacitor-Current">
			  <title>Capacitor Figure 1</title>
			<caption>A capacitor made of two conducting plates.</caption>
			<image source="Capacitor-Current.png" width = "50%" ><description>A horizontal rectangle with point A on the left and D on the right below a second horizontal rectangle with point B on the left and C on the right.</description></image>
		</figure>
	</introduction>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Are the conducting plates in electrostatic equilibrium?
			</p>
		</statement>
	</task>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Sketch a charge diagram for the capacitor.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</task>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Sketch the electric field inside the capacitor.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</task>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Determine the potential at each point.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</task>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Describe all changes in the electric potential as you travel around the path <m>A \rightarrow B \rightarrow C \rightarrow D \rightarrow A</m>.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</task>
	
   </activity>

   <activity>
	<title>Inside a Capacitor II</title>
	<introduction>
		<p>
			
		</p>
		<p>
			Suppose you connect the two plates of the capacitor with a third conductor between points A and B, as in the figure below.
		</p>
		<figure xml:id="Capacitor-Current3">
			  <title>Capacitor Figure 3</title>
			<caption>A capacitor made of two conducting plates, with a third conductor.</caption>
			<image source="Capacitor-Current3.png" width = "50%" ><description>A horizontal rectangle with point A on the left and D on the right below a second horizontal rectangle with point B on the left and C on the right.  The upper rectangle has positive charge distributed horizontally and the lower rectangle has negative charge distributed horizontally.  A uniform downward electric field occupies the space between the rectangles.  A vertical rectangle connects points B and A.</description></image>
		</figure>
	</introduction>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Are the conducting plates in electrostatic equilibrium?
			</p>
		</statement>
	</task>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Describe what you expect to happen physically to the charges (you may want to draw multiple charge diagrams showing the situation over time).
			</p>
		</statement>
	</task>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Give a mechanistic explanation for your expectation based on the electric field or the electric potential.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</task>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				As before, describe all changes in the electric potential as you travel around the path <m>A \rightarrow B \rightarrow C \rightarrow D \rightarrow A</m>.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</task>
	
   </activity>
	
<!--NO AI-->
</section>

